with q as (
  select $astroTitan_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Ganymede is larger and more massive than Titan, yet has essentially no atmosphere while Titan holds a surface pressure of about 1.5 bar. What is the chapter's resolution of this apparent paradox?", "options": ["Titan's greater mass gives it a higher escape velocity than Ganymede, so it retains gas that the smaller Ganymede loses.", "Ganymede's atmosphere was stripped by Jupiter's powerful magnetic field, a hazard Titan escapes because Saturn's field is weaker.", "The decisive factors are temperature and formation history, not size: at 94 K nitrogen's thermal speed is a tiny fraction of Titan's 2.64 km/s escape velocity so Jeans escape is negligible, and Titan formed in the cold outer part of Saturn's disk where it accreted and kept volatile-rich (nitrogen- and carbon-bearing) ices, whereas Ganymede formed warmer and lost its inventory.", "Titan's atmosphere is primordial nebular gas captured during formation, while Ganymede formed too late to capture any."], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "Size actually favors Ganymede (2,634 km radius versus Titan's 2,575 km, and more massive), so mass cannot be the answer. Escape is a thermal process: a molecule leaves only if it is high up moving faster than the escape velocity (2.64 km/s for Titan). At 94 K a nitrogen molecule's typical speed is only a few hundred m/s, roughly a tenth of the escape speed, and because escape depends on the exponentially small tail of the speed distribution, thermal (Jeans) escape of nitrogen is negligible over the age of the solar system. Cold is a lid. Formation location matters too: Titan condensed in the cold outer reaches of Saturn's disk and could gather and retain nitrogen- and carbon-bearing ices, while Ganymede formed in Jupiter's warmer, harsher environment and never built up or kept the same volatile store. The magnetic-field and primordial-capture options are not the chapter's mechanism; the nitrogen was largely outgassed from accreted ammonia ice, not captured from the nebula."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter says Titan runs a full hydrological cycle but 'in the wrong fluid.' What physical coincidence makes methane, rather than water, the working fluid on Titan?", "options": ["At Titan's 94 K and 1.5 bar, methane straddles its phase boundaries (it boils at 112 K and freezes at 91 K), so it can be gas, liquid, and solid at the ambient conditions, while water at 94 K is frozen as hard as granite and forms the bedrock.", "Methane is far more abundant on Titan than water, so it simply dominates every reservoir by mass.", "Water cannot exist on Titan at all because the atmosphere contains no oxygen to form it.", "Sunlight at Saturn's distance is too weak to evaporate water but strong enough to evaporate methane."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Earth's hydrology works because water happens to sit astride its solid-liquid-vapor phase boundaries at ordinary surface conditions, so small temperature changes shuttle it between phases. Titan did not lose that coincidence; it relocated it to a different molecule. At 94 K and 1.5 bar water is frozen rock, forming Titan's crust, cobbles, and mountains, far below any liquid range. Methane, which boils at 112 K and freezes at 91 K at one atmosphere, sits squarely in its liquid range there and only a few degrees above freezing, close to all three phase boundaries. So methane evaporates from the seas, forms clouds, rains, runs downhill carving channels and building deltas, pools into seas, and evaporates again, a complete cycle. Abundance, absence of oxygen, and weak sunlight are not the reason; it is the position of methane's phase boundaries relative to the ambient temperature and pressure."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Solar UV photolysis destroys Titan's atmospheric methane on a replenishment timescale of only about 10 to 30 million years. Why is this a genuine open problem, and what makes the destruction irreversible?", "options": ["The methane simply freezes out onto the surface and can be re-evaporated, so no true resupply is needed.", "Because 10 to 30 Myr is less than one percent of the solar system's 4.5-billion-year age, the methane would have been exhausted more than a hundred times over if unreplenished; it is irreversible because photolysis strips off hydrogen light enough to escape Titan to space, so there is no route back from ethane to methane, meaning the methane must be resupplied from below (cryovolcanism, clathrate reservoirs, or episodic outgassing).", "Photolysis converts methane to nitrogen, which is why the atmosphere is 95 percent nitrogen, and the process reverses seasonally.", "The destruction is balanced exactly by comet impacts delivering fresh methane, so the inventory is known to be in steady state."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The bookkeeping is alarming: dividing the atmospheric methane inventory by the photolysis destruction rate gives a replenishment time of only about 10 to 30 million years, less than one percent of the 4.5-billion-year age of the solar system. Had the methane simply been sitting there since formation, it would have run dry over a hundred times over, and the clouds, rain, and seas would have wound down eons ago. Yet the machine is running now, so methane must be resupplied on a short timescale. The conversion is one-way because photolysis strips hydrogen, which is light enough to escape Titan entirely into Saturn's magnetosphere; once the hydrogen is gone there is no surface route from ethane back to methane. The chapter names three candidate resupply mechanisms, all from below: cryovolcanism, release from crustal clathrate hydrate reservoirs, and episodic outgassing. Which dominates is unknown, and the honest answer is that we do not know. Comet delivery and a methane-to-nitrogen reaction are not the chapter's account."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What are 'tholins,' and why does the chapter argue they command attention out of all proportion to a haze?", "options": ["They are ice crystals of frozen methane that make up Titan's clouds, and they matter because they scatter sunlight and cool the surface.", "They are salts dissolved in the subsurface ocean that act as antifreeze, and they matter because they keep the deep water liquid.", "They are nitrogen ice grains blown into dunes, and they matter because they record Titan's wind patterns.", "They are reddish-brown macromolecular organic solids of carbon, hydrogen, and nitrogen built by UV photolysis of methane and nitrogen; they matter because Titan runs, at planetary scale for billions of years, the prebiotic organic chemistry thought to have preceded life on Earth, making nitriles like hydrogen cyanide by the tonne and raining them onto the surface as a natural laboratory."], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "Tholins (from the Greek for 'muddy,' a term coined by Carl Sagan and Bjorn Reynolds) are the reddish-brown macromolecular organic solids that form when UV light takes apart methane and nitrogen high in the atmosphere and the fragments assemble step by step into ethane, acetylene, hydrogen cyanide and larger nitriles, aromatic rings, and tangled C-H-N solids. The orange haze that hides Titan's surface is this tholin factory in operation; the aerosols coagulate, grow heavy, and rain down, potentially building a global organic layer hundreds of meters thick over the age of the solar system. They command attention because Titan is running, continuously and at the scale of an entire world, the assembly of complex carbon-and-nitrogen molecules from simple feedstock and an energy source, the very chemistry thought to precede life. Nitriles like HCN are exactly the molecules that, given liquid water, polymerize toward amino acids and nucleobases. Titan is thus a natural laboratory for prebiotic chemistry, not merely a hazy moon. The other options confuse tholins with clouds, ocean salts, or dune ice."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter argues that Titan is a 'double target' for astrobiology, and it describes the Huygens landing as evidence for surface hydrology. Which statement correctly combines these points?", "options": ["Huygens landed on 14 January 2005 and photographed rounded water-ice cobbles on a methane-damp plain (signs of past liquid flow); Titan offers two potentially habitable environments, the exotic hydrocarbon-solvent surface and a conventional global subsurface ocean of liquid water inferred from Titan's large tidal flexing.", "Huygens splashed down in Kraken Mare in 2005 and directly sampled living organisms; the two habitable environments are the north and south polar seas.", "Huygens was a Cassini instrument that never separated; the two targets are Titan's atmosphere and its dune fields.", "Huygens landed in 1997 on arrival at Saturn; the subsurface ocean is ruled out because Titan is too rigid to flex under Saturn's tides."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The Huygens probe, part of the joint NASA-ESA Cassini-Huygens mission (launched 1997), descended by parachute over about two and a half hours and touched down on 14 January 2005, the most distant landing ever made, some 1.2 billion km from the Sun. It saw dendritic drainage networks from the air and, on the ground, photographed rounded cobbles of water ice a few centimeters across, whose smoothed shapes are the signature of tumbling in flowing liquid; the surface was damp with methane, a dry floodplain between rains. Separately, Cassini measured that Titan flexes a lot under Saturn's tidal pull (a large Love number), which requires a globally continuous liquid layer at depth, implying a global subsurface ocean of liquid water, probably ammonia-bearing and salty, under 50 to 150 km of ice. That gives Titan two candidate habitable environments: the exotic hydrocarbon-solvent surface with abundant organics, and the conventional water ocean of the same family as those inside Europa and Enceladus. The distractors misstate the landing date, location, and the tidal evidence."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain why the STYLE of methane resupply (steady versus episodic) changes the meaning of everything Cassini mapped, and describe how the Dragonfly mission is designed to address the prebiotic-chemistry and habitability questions this raises, including why it flies rather than rolls.", "rubric": "A strong answer first establishes the setup: methane is destroyed by photolysis on a replenishment timescale of about 10 to 30 million years (far shorter than the 4.5-billion-year age of the solar system), the conversion is irreversible because hydrogen escapes to space, so methane must be resupplied from below, but the mechanism is unknown. It should then explain the two interpretations. If resupply is STEADY, destruction and supply are in balance and Titan's seas, rivers, and dunes are a long-lived, quasi-steady landscape; what Cassini mapped is roughly what Titan has looked like for a very long time. If resupply is EPISODIC (bursts tied to internal or orbital cycles, cryovolcanism, or clathrate release), the atmosphere is charged up in active epochs and runs down between them, so the wet, cloudy, sea-bearing Titan we met could be a passing special epoch in a history that is mostly dry, meaning we arrived at a lucky moment. The same map therefore means either 'normal Titan' or 'lucky glimpse' depending on the resupply style, which is why it is not a detail. On Dragonfly: it is a nuclear-powered rotorcraft (car-sized flying drone), scheduled to launch around 2028 and arrive around 2034, that hops kilometers between sites across the equatorial dunes and plains, analyzing surface composition, how far the photochemical organics/tholins have progressed, whether biochemical building blocks are present, and (potentially near an impact crater where organics may have mixed with liquid water) staying alert for biosignatures; it is a prebiotic-chemistry and habitability mission reading the results of a 4.5-billion-year natural experiment. On why it flies: flight is easy on Titan because the atmosphere near the surface is roughly four times denser than Earth's (more lift per unit rotor) AND gravity is about one-seventh of Earth's (far less weight to hold up); both stack in the flyer's favor, making powered flight easier than anywhere else in the solar system, whereas rolling a rover over unknown soft, sticky organic terrain is risky and slow. A top answer connects the pieces: characterizing the thickness, age, and composition of surface organics and the ethane budget could in principle discriminate steady from episodic history, though it remains unsettled because we have never directly observed the resupply."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Titan's surface gravity is only about 1.35 m/s^2 (roughly one-seventh of Earth's), yet its atmosphere is denser at the surface than Earth's and extends far higher. Explain, using the chapter's reasoning and numbers, how weak gravity produces both a higher surface pressure (about 1.5 bar) and an unusually tall, extended atmosphere.", "rubric": "A strong answer treats two linked effects. FIRST, surface pressure as the weight of the atmospheric column: P = m_col x g. Because Titan's gravity is one-seventh of Earth's, each kilogram of gas presses down more gently, so to reach 1.5 bar Titan must pile up far MORE mass per unit area than Earth needs for 1.0 bar, on the order of 110 tonnes per square meter versus about 10 for Earth (roughly eleven times more air per square meter). The key insight: low gravity does not mean thin air; it is precisely what lets a small world accumulate an outsized mass of gas without producing crushing pressures. The answer should note the nitrogen composition (about 95-98 percent N2, the only nitrogen-dominated atmosphere besides Earth's, roughly 5 percent methane near the surface) and that the nitrogen was largely outgassed from accreted ammonia. SECOND, vertical extent via the scale height H = kT/(mu m_H g), the height over which pressure falls by a factor e. Plugging T = 94 K, mu = 28, g = 1.35 gives H near 20 km, several times Earth's ~8 km, DESPITE Titan being three times colder, because H scales as 1/g and weak gravity multiplies H by about seven, overturning the roughly three-fold shrinkage from being colder. The tall scale height lofts the haze to 500-600 km, some 24 scale heights and a large fraction of Titan's 2,575 km radius, making Titan's atmosphere proportionally the most extended of any known world, which is why the haze hid the surface from telescopes and Voyager. A top answer ties both effects to the same cause, weak gravity, and may note the striking consequence that Titan's TOTAL atmospheric mass (about 9 x 10^18 kg) rivals or exceeds Earth's (5.1 x 10^18 kg) despite Titan being a moon smaller than Mars, because eleven times the column mass on roughly a quarter of the surface area still nets a comparable or larger total."}]$astroTitan_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
